SM(222,{"n":"Genesis 2.8-25","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Genesis 2.8-25.mp3","d":2372.6,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[19.76,"So verse 8."],[21.2,"Then the Lord God planted a garden eastward in Eden, and there he put the man he formed."],[30.96,"This is easy to gloss over."],[34.56,"But first of all, God creates Eden, and it is in a particular place in relation to the rest of the world."],[44.72,"Eden is not the whole world."],[49.33,"Eden is a particular garden in a particular place."],[53.01,"God creates the garden."],[55.27,"In that um in that particular place."],[59.95,"Now the other name for it is um I don't think."],[69.47,"That's a shame."],[71.07,"Uh doesn't look like the Orthodox Study Bible they actually used the Greek word paradise, um, which is literally the Greek word that's here."],[79.71,"Um and I use the term Greek word kind of loosely because it's actually a loan word from Persian."],[89.55,"And so what a a paradise was was it was a particular type of walled garden."],[98.67,"It was very popular in Persia, both the old Persian and the later Persian. uh empires"],[106.87,"And if you want to see an example of a paradise that is still standing today, it is the Taj Mahal, not the hotel casino, the uh original one."],[118.51,"Which was built in the Persian style."],[121.63,"Um, and as we'll see in a minute here, um, if you actually look at an aerial photo of the Taj Mahal, uh everybody just thinks of the building."],[135.65,"Not the garden."],[136.93,"That's the walled garden that's behind it."],[139.11,"But that's the actual paradise is the walled garden that's behind it."],[142.55,"You have the building that represents sort of the mountain of God."],[145.79,"Then behind that you have the walled garden, and in it, it's transected by four waterways that radiate out from a center point."],[156.11,"We're gonna see Eden is described exactly the same way."],[162.48,"But again, the description of it as a walled garden again means this is a particular locale that is separated from the rest of the world."],[173.36,"Yes, sir. What does the word Eden perch in is Hebrew?"],[178.48,"Why does it literally mean?"],[180.56,"Um, it's unclear."],[184.16,"So there are people who theorize it's a place, it's a literal place name."],[188.8,"I do not."],[190.4,"Um that's because it literally in the Hebrews said well, it's they translate that way here too."],[196.04,"A garden eastward in Eden."],[198.46,"So Eden is not the name of the garden."],[201.66,"It's the garden of Eden because it's a garden, and that Eden is the place where it is."],[209.5,"Um yeah. And I promise I will not belabor this a bunch more because I've already done it enough."],[221.74,"But you will see countless documentaries on the History Channel and Discovery Channel and other places if you're like me and feel compelled to watch horrible Bible documentaries where they try to pinpoint the exact place where this is based on this, that, and the other."],[238.58,"And it's a waste of time."],[242.83,"And we'll see more of why as we go on."],[245.55,"But this is literally being described as not a literal place in this world at the present time."],[256.75,"Our present. So Eden later on, that I mentioned the mountain of God in terms of the Taj Mahal."],[266.82,"Eden's gonna be mentioned as being on the mountain of God in Ezekiel."],[272.18,"And paradise or this garden is the place where God dwells."],[278.66,"And this is common imagery in terms of Moses' vision."],[283.84,"This is common imagery for the ancient Near East."],[286.72,"The ancient Near East gods lived on mountains."],[289.46,"I mean, Mount Olympus being the latest example of that."],[292.94,"Um, they lived on mountains and they lived in gardens."],[297.5,"And you can imagine why that was in the ancient Near East."],[300.32,"You go and look at a rock."],[303.57,"If you live in a rock, a uh a garden on a mountain."],[309.03,"A garden where it's cool sounds pretty good."],[311.81,"Like if you're living in Mesopotamia."],[314.21,"Um that's where gods lived, and even in tents, but we'll talk about that more when we get to the tabernacle in Exodus."],[320.85,"Um that same imagery is being used here."],[324.81,"So this is the place where this garden is the place where God dwells."],[330.34,"Where he's gonna dwell with man."],[333.46,"But again, because this is a garden that is not the whole world, that is a walled garden and therefore separated from the world."],[342.68,"And is already a garden."],[345.48,"What did three verses ago, what did it say was one of the reasons humanity was created."],[352.0,"So the ground."],[353.16,"To work the ground."],[354.28,"Well, you don't need to work the ground inside a garden that's already a cultivated garden."],[360.52,"You have to do that outside."],[364.34,"So we remember talked about the fill the earth and subdue it language, in Genesis 1."],[371.6,"To continue to go out and put the world into order and fill it with life."],[377.9,"With a garden being the perfect example of those two things together."],[382.31,"As we said last time, swamp is full of life, no order."],[387.45,"Just chaos that makes it dangerous."],[389.9,"Parking lot is uh a brand new parking lot is in good order, but there's no life."],[397.84,"And so the garden is the place where those two things meet."],[402.66,"It's the place where those two things meet."],[404.42,"And so humanity is not being created to stay in the garden forever and just enjoy the nice garden."],[413.82,"You've already been tipped off in these verses we just read."],[417.18,"Humanity is being created to eventually, eventually, at some certain point, leave the garden and take the garden with them to cultivate the world into a garden by cooperating with God."],[432.94,"Spoilers, that's not what's gonna happen."],[435.26,"But right, but that's right, the the sort of stated intent here."],[441.51,"Notice also Adam is not created in the garden."],[445.95,"God knows I never know."],[447.91,"He's not created in paradise"],[449.95,"He's created in the world that he's going to be sent back into and then taken and put in paradise, put in the garden."],[459.44,"So he's brought into the presence of God."],[463.54,"God takes him to dwell with him."],[466.9,"Which tells us that what's going on in the garden is supposed to be preparatory."],[474.36,"Preparatory. There's something else coming already."],[477.96,"So this is not God intended for humanity to live in the garden with him forever, but we blew it, and so now plan B."],[488.17,"Christ and everything else."],[490.25,"There', and this also means humanity isnt created perfect."],[498.89,"It means humanity is created innocent."],[503.61,"Humanity is created with a destiny beyond the point where he's created."],[509.69,"So salvation is not getting back to where Adam was."],[515.77,"It's accomplishing what Adam was created to accomplish."],[528.49,"Number nine. Besides this, God caused every tree beautiful to the sight and good for food to grow from the ground."],[535.37,"Also in the middle of of the garden were the tree of life and the tree of learning the knowledge good and evil."],[543.73,"So it's full of trees."],[545.41,"I think we mentioned this last time, but what's great about a tree?"],[551.97,"A tree feeds you and you don't have to kill it."],[557.78,"Even with plants that grow out of the ground."],[560.36,"You gotta you gotta rip up the rutabaga if you're gonna eat it."],[563.66,"Rutabaga has to die."],[565.26,"But with a tree, nothing has to die for you to eat fruit from a tree."],[569.3,"So fruit trees represent nature and the world offering itself to humanity for food to sustain him."],[577.34,"And this kind of cooperation."],[586.26,"One is the tree of life, and one is the tree of the knowledge of good and evil."],[594.26,"Verse 10. Now a river went out of Eden to water the garden, and from there it separated into four heads."],[604.5,"The name of the first is Pishon."],[606.98,"It circles the land of Havila, where there is gold."],[610.18,"And the gold of that land is good."],[613.38,"You don't want bad gold."],[616.34,"The carbuncle and the emerald are there as well."],[619.7,"The name of the second river is Gihon, it circles all the land of Ethiopia."],[623.86,"The name of the third river is Tigris."],[626.3,"It flows over against the Assyrians."],[628.7,"And the fourth river is Euphrates."],[638.12,"When they translated it into Greek from which this is translated, they uh they nailed down a couple of them as the Tigris and Euphrates."],[647.24,"It's pretty clear the Hebrew that that's what they are."],[649.4,"It's just those names don't appear."],[652.05,"In the way that they do they do here."],[656.53,"But so even just based on what's here, I know that first one we're gonna have to come back to."],[663.78,"Because where? What?"],[665.46,"Havilah, huh? But we've got the Tigris and the Euphrates."],[671.34,"They're the two rivers that frame Mesopotamia."],[673.58,"That's why it's called Mesopotamia, the land between the rivers."],[676.96,"They flow into the Persian Gulf."],[680.52,"Then we have this river that flows from Ethiopia."],[685.88,"Meaning, what river would that be?"],[688.92,"The Nile. It starts at Ethiopia and flows north up to the Nile Delta."],[696.57,"So, before we even get into the fourth one, does anybody see an issue with trying to identify where on present-day Earth the Garden of Eden is, that the Nile, the Tigris, and the Euphrates all flow out of it."],[714.85,"Can't read it."],[716.59,"It's geographically impossible."],[719.09,"Even with just those three."],[721.33,"Now, in terms of the uh the first one that's listed, this gets identified in a bunch of different ways."],[730.26,"And even different church fathers identify it in different ways."],[736.11,"And this is because church fathers, like everyone else, were reading the text and doing their best to figure it out."],[743.55,"Sometimes some Orthodox folks talk about the church fathers like they were all magic oracles who like could just look at the biblical text and somehow mystically understand exactly what it meant in a way that we mere mortals cannot."],[759.35,"Um but they're doing their best."],[761.91,"That's why they disagree about this, because it's kind of unclear."],[764.87,"Personally, I go with what at least a couple of church fathers say."],[768.55,"I think it's supposed to be the Danube."],[771.91,"Yes. Which likewise, and see, most of the people who don't want it to be the Danube are trying to make it fit to get you an actual geographical location, but I'm like, you can't get one from the other three, so why forget it."],[787.73,"Geography has changed so much."],[789.59,"And I'm thinking you know the Ethiopian church, um, don't they say Ethiopia is you know somewhere in that um area uh Ethiopia I I don't I don't know for sure I know that Ethiopian Jews inherited the tradition the general tradition of Judaism that it's it was where Jerusalem is which is actually the overriding tradition and the tradition we find when we get to the New Testament is that um and in our own Orthodox tradition, that and obviously that can't be literal."],[832.73,"Because none of these rivers flow out of Jerusalem."],[835.78,"There aren't four rivers flowing out of Jerusalem, period."],[839.22,"Um, but that's what was taken to be, and that's that's the whole tradition of Jerusalem as the navel of the world."],[846.84,"It's the center of the world."],[848.12,"That's why the place where Christ was crucified was called the Hill of the Skull, is because they believed Adam was buried there."],[853.88,"It was outside of Jerusalem at the time, and so if Jerusalem was Eden, he's buried outside of Eden."],[859.9,"Um if you go to the Church of the Holy Sepulchre and you go down below, there is a shrine of Adam at that traditional burial place."],[872.82,"Um, and so that becomes really well documented Jewish tradition."],[877.64,"But if you're trying to take this literally to find a geographic point, you're not gonna get that from these four rivers."],[883.16,"Yeah Well, aren't these orders like the center of like basically all civilization?"],[887.6,"Yes. And so there are some people, um a little later fathers who want to identify that first one as the Indus."],[897.92,"For that reason."],[898.8,"Yeah. For that reason."],[902.16,"Wouldn't that lead us to think that this is more saying more about that kind of idea?"],[909.58,"Exactly. Exactly. It's it's not trying to give a physical location for us to go and take a pilgrimage to."],[917.98,"I would think that the ancient Near Eastern people would be aware of the Indus."],[923.18,"I can't see how they would be aware of the denity."],[927.02,"Well, it depends on. a couple things"],[931.7,"But it's possible."],[932.83,"But the point is we don't even need to nail it down that precisely."],[937.07,"But let me let me make another point."],[938.53,"If we have four rivers flowing out of somewhere in four different directions, what would that location have to be?"],[948.61,"Have to be a mountain."],[951.31,"If it's because water flows downhill."],[953.71,"So if it's in four different directions going downhill, you have to be on a mountain."],[957.81,"So the idea here is that this water is coming out of paradise, coming out from the mountain of God and flowing out to water and bring life, to the world."],[972.95,"And so all of these rivers where civilization starts, are connected to God's sustenance."],[981.79,"Of the world of humanity."],[984.11,"That's that's the core sort of idea here."],[987.49,"Not for us to get GPS coordinates for the Garden of Eden."],[992.31,"Which could have been given if that was the intent."],[995.96,"Could have been very specific."],[997.96,"So would the head of the river be the fountain that was mentioned?"],[1003.4,"Yes. Okay. The fountain that came up out of the ground."],[1008.86,"And so remember, what were the two problems?"],[1013.22,"No water. No people."],[1016.66,"So if the water is flowing out of Eden into the rest of the world, where is humanity gonna go?"],[1024.67,"Like the same thing."],[1026.61,"Um this imagery of the water, Saint John is gonna do all kinds of things with this."],[1036.45,"St. John's gospel is full of Genesis stuff."],[1040.87,"But when Christ talks about um giving people water, water springing up to eternal life, uh, when he says, He to whom I give to drink will have uh rivers of water flowing out of his belly, and you're like, that sounds like dysentery."],[1061.27,"He's he's what he's aiming at is that person will become paradise."],[1066.31,"We'll have paradise within them."],[1069.45,"God will come to dwell within them."],[1071.37,"The place where God dwells is paradise."],[1075.05,"Verse 15. Then the Lord took the man he formed and put him in the garden to tend and keep it."],[1083.05,"So this is this first stage."],[1086.62,"Well, start you out, you're gonna tend the garden I planted."],[1090.88,"Once you learn to do that, you go out."],[1096.26,"And the Lord God commanded Adam saying, You may eat food from every tree in the garden, but from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, you may not eat."],[1107.02,"For on whatever day you eat from it, you shall die by death, which is a very literal kind of translation of uh of the Hebrew."],[1116.78,"The Hebrew is sort of literally dying, you will die."],[1119.74,"This is construction called an infinitive absolute."],[1122.94,"Um, or you'll die to death."],[1127.1,"Um famously, that's the you shall surely die in the King James."],[1132.98,"Um, look, all of these trees."],[1140.12,"Doesn' sayt anything about the tree of life here."],[1142.48,"Notice. That'll come back in chapter three."],[1145.92,"He says, but don't eat from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, because in the day you do it, you'll surely die."],[1152.24,"Now, what does he not say here?"],[1156.88,"He does not say, if you disobey me and eat from that tree, I will kill you."],[1166.48,"Is death presented here as a penalty?"],[1170.8,"It's just a consequence."],[1172.48,"Or is it a warning?"],[1175.81,"Don't put the fork in the light socket."],[1179.17,"Because you uh the day you do that, you will surely be electrocuted."],[1184.01,"I'm telling them what the consequence will be of that action."],[1184.19,"I'm not threatening to electrocute the person if they disobey me."],[1198.43,"And the tree of the knowledge of good and evil."],[1202.41,"Every other place in Scripture where the phrase, the knowledge of good and evil, appears."],[1211.22,"And there are a couple."],[1213.38,"One of them is when Solomon is asked what he wants as a gift from God, he says he wants the knowledge of good and evil."],[1222.42,"That's usually recorded as yes for wisdom."],[1225.94,"Another one is in Isaiah, in the famous prophecy about the virgin or the young woman giving birth to a child, uh, where uh it says, before that child is old enough to choose the good and reject the evil."],[1246.03,"This will happen."],[1247.39,"Every place else where that phrase occurs, it refers to maturity, specifically mental maturity."],[1257.2,"Uh it refers to what becomes in sort of Western thought referred to as the age of accountability."],[1264.37,"So, you know, you're around kids, you realize up until a certain age, they really don't understand like the consequences of their actions."],[1276.59,"They really don't understand if I put the fork in the light socket, I will get electrocuted."],[1282.12,"And then there's a certain point where they come to maturity and can begin to understand that."],[1289.9,"That's when they acquire the knowledge of good and evil."],[1293.42,"So if that's how that phrase is used in Hebrew, if that's what the Hebrew idiom means, what's going on here?"],[1299.9,"Adam is being presented, he just got created."],[1304.43,"So he may be physically an adult male human."],[1308.25,"Maybe. I'm not gonna get into the whole argument about whether he had a navel."],[1311.77,"I'm not going there."],[1313.09,"Um, but you know, he was born yesterday."],[1320.47,"He's he is not really sure he does not know these things."],[1324.83,"And God is telling him, you don't need to run ahead and try and grab that maturity for yourself."],[1334.91,"When you look at Second Temple Jewish literature and you look at the church fathers, they present the tree of the knowledge of good and evil as still being in paradise."],[1348.99,"It is not evil."],[1350.07,"Nothing God created is evil."],[1351.63,"That tree is not evil."],[1356.77,"The problem is Adam and his soon to exist wife, take from it before the time when they're ready."],[1370.82,"Before they're able to use that knowledge in a responsible way."],[1377.14,"When the devil shows up, and we'll talk more about him next time when he shows up."],[1381.2,"Why does he want them to do it?"],[1383.44,"He wants to destroy them. that knowledge before they're ready and understand how to use it"],[1395.2,"This is an ongoing theme in Genesis."],[1398.72,"That knowledge, technology. the innovations of culture are not good in and of themselves"],[1407.72,"They can be used for good when a certain type of person who is properly prepared receives them and uses them that way."],[1419.0,"Or they could be incredibly destructive."],[1424.36,"Don't hand a five-year-old a firearm."],[1429.8,"Not because guns are evil, but because a five-year-old can't use one responsibly."],[1436.28,"He's going to hurt themselves or someone else"],[1439.62,"And knowledge functions the same way."],[1443.38,"And so this is a warning."],[1446.87,"Adam is here being portrayed as, as he'll later be listed in uh one of Christ's genealogies, as it so happens, uh, as the Son of God."],[1460.39,"Meaning child. Meaning child."],[1467.49,"He's being prepared for something."],[1475.86,"Verse 18. And the Lord God said, It is not good for man to be alone."],[1482.02,"I will make him a helper comparable to him."],[1488.7,"Well, continue. Also, God formed out of the ground all the wild animals of the field and all the birds of heaven, and brought them to Adam to see what he would call them."],[1498.54,"Thus, whatever Adam called each living creature, that was its name."],[1503.66,"So Adam gave names to all the cattle, to all the birds of heaven, and to all the wild animals of the field."],[1509.74,"But for Adam there was not found a helper comparable to him."],[1521.22,"Let's talk about this."],[1522.58,"Because this is sometimes seen as a in in a silly way."],[1527.74,"So and there's a couple problems here with the way we read even that first verse."],[1532.62,"So it's interesting that this is the first time God has said that something isn't good"],[1539.42,"Everything else he created, he looked at and he said it was good."],[1542.46,"But now he says, wait, not good for a man to be alone."],[1550.26,"So a problem we have here as modern readers, uh, as people uh passed the ideals of romantic love."],[1562.6,"Is that we look at this and say, oh, the problem is Adam is lonely."],[1568.46,"He's there, he's all by himself, he feels sad."],[1571.82,"He's lonely. Well, what's the problem with that?"],[1579.14,"He's living with God."],[1586.27,"Exactly. Yeah, he doesn't miss having a woman around."],[1590.67,"There aren't any."],[1592.14,"And he's got God."],[1594.62,"He's not alone."],[1596.84,"He's living with God."],[1600.28,"Okay. So that sort of psychologized, romanticized version of this."],[1606.58,"What does it mean that it's not good that he's alone?"],[1608.66,"It's not good that he's the only human."],[1611.22,"There need to be more humans."],[1612.82,"Well, how are they going to get to be more humans?"],[1615.94,"I hope I'm not breaking news to anybody."],[1619.96,"In order to get more humans, you need a male human and a female human."],[1625.42,"I'm canceled now."],[1626.86,"I know. Biological male and a biological female."],[1632.51,"Um to produce more humans."],[1635.95,"So that's the problem."],[1639.23,"Also, another weird way."],[1641.89,"There's a lot of starting here and going well into chapter three with the snake."],[1646.25,"Th'eres a lot of rudyard Kipling that gets read into this, too."],[1649.25,"You know, is this kind of quaint story of like, as if Adam's like looking at all the animals trying to figure out which one he should mate with?"],[1658.05,"It's like, go figure, I checked out all the animals, and none of them I can mate with."],[1663.05,"Duh. Like, I mean, I said he was innocent like a child, but he's not an idiot."],[1668.21,"Like, and God certainly isn't."],[1671.99,"So that's not what's going on with the naming of the animals."],[1676.07,"Okay. What did we say coming out of chapter one?"],[1681.44,"Setting things in order, filling them with life."],[1684.42,"And that humanity is going to continue these two tasks."],[1687.94,"Okay. So we have this problem."],[1692.5,"Well, he can't fill the world with life because he can't reproduce."],[1698.95,"Can't just reproduce by butting or something."],[1701.69,"Um, and now we have this thing with the naming of the animals."],[1708.97,"So, one of the interesting things we found archaeologically, and we found this in Hittite ruins, are relief images of various Hittite kings sitting on a throne, holding a rod, naming animals that are walking past in front of them."],[1735.31,"And this imagery was common ancient imagery to describe sort of authority."],[1743.72,"The king.'s job was to put things in order and to maintain order"],[1747.69,"Wild animals are the symbol of disorder."],[1751.71,"And chaos. So the king is there putting these wild animals in order."],[1759.33,"By naming them, by putting them into a taxonomy."],[1762.67,"He's applying order to the sort of wild parts of the world, civilizing it in our modern terms."],[1770.39,"That's the image."],[1772.15,"So Adam can't be fruitful and multiply and fill the earth yet."],[1779.03,"But he's getting started on the subduing it part."],[1783.96,"And notice God is involved."],[1785.56,"This is in that cooperative sense"],[1785.76,"God is watching him do this."],[1790.12,"So this is exercise number one about putting the world in order."],[1794.6,"Adam is carrying out."],[1796.52,"Okay. And after he's done with that, now we're gonna see, okay, now we need to address that other problem."],[1802.46,"About making more people."],[1808.94,"Verse 21. This God brought a trance upon Adam, and he slept, and he took one of his ribs and filled up the flesh in its place."],[1818.62,"Okay. Got a little more kipling going on here, I think."],[1823.34,"So this whole rib thing, I'm gonna ruin your Sunday school."],[1826.86,"This is another episode of Father Stephen Ruins Your Sunday School."],[1829.1,"Okay There's nothing about a rib anywhere in this story."],[1835.3,"So the image here, and what's translated as trance, it's kind of ambiguous whether Adam is alive or dead."],[1848.34,"Trance here is more like coma, maybe, or vegetative state."],[1852.91,"He's out. And this is so clear in the original, and even in the Greek, actually, that the church fathers often comment on this and make comparisons between Christ and the tomb and Adam."],[1867.97,"And then uh the woman and the church, and right make those kind of so the idea that he's out."],[1873.25,"'s And the word that's used here that translated open up the side."],[1881.04,"And translated as rib, um, is actually side as in like dorsal ventral or sides as in halves of a building."],[1891.92,"Those are the other uses it's put to, in scripture."],[1895.63,"So you have like the one side of the building, the other side of the building, like that are symmetrical, but the opposite halves."],[1901.25,"So the idea here is that Adam gets pulled in two."],[1910.83,"Ripped in half."],[1912.97,"Pulled apart. And then once ripped apart, God sort of fills in the other half."],[1926.34,"In verse 22, then the Lord God built the rib he took from Adam into a woman and brought her to him."],[1932.58,"So he takes the half that and builds that out too."],[1935.3,"So both halves get built out."],[1937.38,"And now you have man and woman."],[1939.87,"Which in Hebrew are Esh and Esha."],[1944.8,"Because he shah means from man."],[1947.76,"But the idea here is He gets pulled in two, man and woman."],[1953.52,"And now, as we're going to see, the idea is that those two come back together."],[1957.92,"In marriage And when they come back together, they produce new life."],[1966.44,"In the world."],[1972.84,"Verse 23. So Adam said, This is now bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh."],[1978.84,"Like literally. That part's kind of literal based on the description of what happened."],[1986.92,"She shall be called woman because she was taken out of man."],[1992.28,"For this reason, a man shall leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and the two shall become one flesh."],[2003.28,"So that's why this is a physical reunion."],[2008.32,"This idea of one flesh."],[2010.38,"Is that in some sense, the two have been pulled apart and now come back together."],[2021.95,"Become one flesh, become one together."],[2026.75,"And this is what Christ is going to appeal to he him and say,y, uh Moses said it was okay for us to divorce our wives as long as we give them a writ of divorce."],[2044.76,"And that was as opposed to abandoning them."],[2050.24,"That was like Moses' point."],[2052.02,"It was like you can't just abandon them and leave them in this limbo where they can't get married again or they can't do anything because they're still technically married to you."],[2062.28,"And since polygamy was allowed, the man could go and do whatever."],[2065.82,"But the woman would be stuck in this limbo place."],[2068.86,"So Moses said, No, if you're gonna divorce them, you have to give her a writ of divorce so she can move on with her life."],[2077.14,"Um Christ says, Yeah, Moses let you do that because of the hardness of your hearts."],[2082.56,"That was not, he says, how it was meant to be from the beginning."],[2086.98,"It says, the two will become one flesh."],[2089.66,"He says, what God has joined together, let no man put asunder."],[2094.86,"So Christ compares divorce to that connection."],[2100.78,"These two people have come together and become one, being ripped apart again."],[2107.5,"That it is an unpleasant, a horrible experience."],[2117.66,"But just as a side note, how does that present divorce?"],[2121.42,"It doesn't present divorce as you broke a rule."],[2126.94,"Now you're going to get punished."],[2128.86,"That presents divorce as this horrible racking experience of being pulled apart."],[2132.38,"Which is often is"],[2135.54,"Yes, which God would like to spare us."],[2139.44,"That experience. But there's a c there are consequences to that."],[2144.34,"And that's why in the Orthodox Church, unlike some other churches that I won't mention by name, when people are divorced, there's a period of quote unquote penance."],[2156.18,"But that's a period of healing and of working through the damage that's been done."],[2163.87,"And and assimilating and bringing those people back into the community."],[2168.37,"At which point they can then take the Eucharist again for the rest of their life."],[2172.85,"Unlike certain other chur.ch, which I won't directly name"],[2180.81,"Because it's a question of healing and injury."],[2186.65,"Not of enforcing a rule or punishing an infraction."],[2194.89,"And the same is true, frankly, although difficult to heal with people who are widowed."],[2203.47,"That that often I've not had this experience by the grace of God."],[2209.15,"I hope I won't."],[2210.43,"The way I eat, it's very unlikely I will outlive my wife."],[2219.43,"But that experience is very much like being torn apart."],[2225.73,"That the idea of two people becoming one flesh is not just a nice image."],[2232.61,"But it reflects a reality in how humans are built, and that's a reality that's being described here in this imagery of the creation of humanity."],[2247.09,"Verse 25, now the two were naked, both Adam and his wife, and we' notre ashamed."],[2255.73,"So there is no concept of shame."],[2257.73,"This is again an image of that innocence."],[2263.5,"Little kids, babies and stuff will run around naked, around naked, reach a certain age, no longer appropriate."],[2274.62,"You've thrown out of a circle K."],[2276.14,"No, I'm just right."],[2279.42,"But it becomes no longer appropriate."],[2282.19,"That's not because there's something evil about reaching a certain age, or something about nudity that's gross or whatever."],[2291.93,"But there is a period of innocence again, and then you come to the knowledge of good and evil."],[2298.59,"You come to maturity."],[2299.71,"There is this thing that is crossed, and it's not sort of this firm line that we can pin down with this certain age, but it's a reality that we all see."],[2308.39,"So this is again an image of innocence for both of them."],[2313.29,"That they are both at sort of the beginning of this sort of journey."],[2318.49,"And unfortunately, this ends up being the setup for what happens next chapter, which probably most people know."],[2324.89,"Although uh when we talk about it next week, I think we're gonna find it's a little different, maybe, when we read the actual text than how it's commonly presented."],[2338.81,"It is not the just so story about how the snake lost its legs."],[2342.25,"We'll put it that way."],[2343.69,"But we will pick up with that next week, Lord Willing"],[2348.17,"So thank you, everybody."],[2350.25,"Listen next time. as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God"],[2357.57,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2362.93,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":19.76,"lv":2,"h":"2:8 - A garden eastward in Eden","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.8</b>: the Lord God planted a <b>garden eastward in Eden</b>, put the man he formed there</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Easy to gloss over\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God creates Eden in a <b>particular place</b> in relation to the rest of the world</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Eden is not the whole world</b> - a particular garden in a particular place</li><li class=\"d0\">Other name: <b>paradise</b> - the Greek word here (Orthodox Study Bible used it)</li><li class=\"d1\">actually a <b>loan word from Persian</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a particular type of <b>walled garden</b> - popular in old and later <b>Persian empires</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Standing example: the <b>Taj Mahal</b> - \"not the hotel casino, the original\" - Persian style</li><li class=\"d1\">aerial photo: everybody thinks of the <b>building</b>, not the garden</li><li class=\"d1\">building = the <b>mountain of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">behind it the <b>walled garden</b>, transected by <b>four waterways</b> radiating from a center point</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Eden is described exactly the same way\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Walled garden = a <b>locale separated from the rest of the world</b></li></ul>"},{"t":173.36,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: the word \"Eden\"; pinpointing the place","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: what does <i>Eden</i> literally mean in Hebrew? <b>Unclear</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some theorize a literal <b>place name</b></li><li class=\"d1\">text: \"a garden <b>eastward in Eden</b>\" - Eden is <b>not the garden's name</b>; it's the place where the garden is</li><li class=\"d0\">Promise not to belabor: <b>History Channel / Discovery Channel</b> documentaries pinpointing the exact place - \"<b>a waste of time</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"horrible Bible documentaries\"</li><li class=\"d0\">It's being described as <b>not a literal place in this world at the present time</b> - more why as we go</li></ul>"},{"t":256.75,"lv":3,"h":"Mountain of God and gardens","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ezekiel</b>: Eden on the <b>mountain of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">paradise = the place where <b>God dwells</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Common imagery in <b>Moses' vision</b> and the <b>ancient Near East</b></li><li class=\"d1\">gods lived on <b>mountains</b> - <b>Mount Olympus</b> the latest example</li><li class=\"d1\">and in <b>gardens</b> - a cool garden on a mountain sounds good if you live in <b>Mesopotamia</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even in <b>tents</b> - more at the <b>tabernacle</b> in Exodus</li><li class=\"d0\">Same imagery here: the garden = where God dwells, <b>where he'll dwell with man</b></li></ul>"},{"t":342.68,"lv":2,"h":"Made to leave the garden","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Garden = <b>not the whole world</b>, <b>walled</b>, separated - and <b>already cultivated</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.5</b> said humanity made in part <b>to work the ground</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no need to work ground in an already cultivated garden - that's <b>outside</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Recall <b>fill the earth and subdue it</b>: go out, <b>put the world in order</b>, fill it with life</li><li class=\"d1\">garden = perfect example of the two together</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>swamp</b>: life, no order, dangerous; new <b>parking lot</b>: order, no life</li><li class=\"d0\">Humanity not created to stay in the garden forever \"and enjoy the nice garden\"</li><li class=\"d1\">tipped off already: eventually <b>leave the garden and take it with them</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>cultivate the world into a garden</b> by cooperating with God</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Spoilers, that's not what's gonna happen\" - but the stated intent</li></ul>"},{"t":441.51,"lv":3,"h":"Adam created outside; innocent, not perfect","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Adam not created in the garden</b>, not in paradise</li><li class=\"d1\">created in the <b>world</b> he'll be sent back into</li><li class=\"d1\">then <b>taken and put</b> in paradise - brought into God's presence, dwells with him</li><li class=\"d0\">Garden is <b>preparatory</b> - something else coming</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"God intended us to live in the garden forever, but we blew it, so <b>plan B</b>: Christ and everything else\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Humanity not created <b>perfect</b> - created <b>innocent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">created with a <b>destiny beyond</b> where he's created</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Salvation</b> is not getting back to where Adam was</li><li class=\"d1\">it's <b>accomplishing what Adam was created to accomplish</b></li></ul>"},{"t":528.49,"lv":2,"h":"2:9 - The trees","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.9</b>: every tree <b>beautiful to the sight and good for food</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in the middle: the <b>tree of life</b> and the <b>tree of the knowledge of good and evil</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Full of trees - what's great about a tree?</li><li class=\"d1\">feeds you and you <b>don't have to kill it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">plants from the ground: \"rip up the <b>rutabaga</b> - rutabaga has to die\"</li><li class=\"d1\">fruit trees = nature and world <b>offering itself</b> to humanity for food - <b>cooperation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two special trees: <b>life</b> and <b>knowledge of good and evil</b></li></ul>"},{"t":594.26,"lv":2,"h":"2:10-14 - Four rivers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.10</b>: a river goes out of Eden to <b>water the garden</b>, then <b>four heads</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Pishon</b> - circles <b>Havilah</b>, gold (\"you don't want bad gold\"), carbuncle, emerald</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Gihon</b> - circles all of <b>Ethiopia</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Tigris</b> - flows toward the <b>Assyrians</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Euphrates</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Greek translators nailed down Tigris and Euphrates - clear in Hebrew</li><li class=\"d0\">First one - \"come back to it\" - \"Havilah, huh?\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Tigris and Euphrates</b> frame <b>Mesopotamia</b> = land between the rivers; flow into the <b>Persian Gulf</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A river from <b>Ethiopia</b> = the <b>Nile</b> - starts in Ethiopia, flows north to the Delta</li><li class=\"d0\">Does anyone see a problem placing Eden on present-day earth? <b>Nile, Tigris, Euphrates all flow out of it</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>geographically impossible</b> - even with just those three</li></ul>"},{"t":721.33,"lv":3,"h":"The first river; Danube; Jerusalem as navel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">First river identified many ways - <b>church fathers differ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fathers read and did their best, like everyone else</li><li class=\"d1\">some Orthodox talk of fathers as \"<b>magic oracles</b>\" who mystically understand - \"they're doing their best\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Personal view, with a couple of fathers: supposed to be the <b>Danube</b></li><li class=\"d1\">those who resist are trying to get an <b>actual geographical location</b> - can't from the other three</li><li class=\"d1\">geography has changed a lot</li><li class=\"d0\">Audience: Ethiopian Jews, Jerusalem tradition</li><li class=\"d1\">overriding <b>Jewish tradition</b>, NT, Orthodox: Eden = <b>Jerusalem</b>, \"<b>navel of the world</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can't be literal - <b>no four rivers</b> out of Jerusalem</li><li class=\"d1\">why Christ crucified at the <b>Hill of the Skull</b>: <b>Adam buried</b> there - outside Jerusalem = outside Eden</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Church of the Holy Sepulchre</b>: <b>shrine of Adam</b> below, traditional burial place</li><li class=\"d1\">well-documented Jewish tradition</li><li class=\"d0\">Taking it literally to get a point from four rivers: \"not gonna get that\"</li></ul>"},{"t":883.16,"lv":3,"h":"Rivers of civilization; water from the mountain","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: rivers = center of basically all <b>civilization</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">yes - later fathers identify the first as the <b>Indus</b> for that reason</li><li class=\"d1\">→ more about that idea; not a <b>physical location for a pilgrimage</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Four rivers flowing four directions → source must be a <b>mountain</b> - water flows downhill</li><li class=\"d1\">water coming out of <b>paradise, the mountain of God</b>, to bring life to the world</li><li class=\"d1\">rivers where civilization starts connected to <b>God's sustenance of humanity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">core idea - not <b>GPS coordinates</b> (could have been given if intended)</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: head of the river = the <b>fountain</b> of v.6? <b>Yes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">two problems: <b>no water, no people</b> - water flows out; where will humanity go?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. John</b>'s gospel \"full of Genesis stuff\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ: water springing up to eternal life; \"<b>rivers of water flowing out of his belly</b>\" - \"sounds like dysentery\"</li><li class=\"d1\">aiming at: that person <b>becomes paradise</b> - <b>God dwells within them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">place where God dwells = paradise</li></ul>"},{"t":1075.05,"lv":2,"h":"2:15-17 - Tend and keep; the command","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.15</b>: the Lord took the man and put him in the garden <b>to tend and keep it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first stage: \"tend the garden I planted; once you learn that, you <b>go out</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.16-17</b>: eat from <b>every tree</b>; not the tree of the knowledge of good and evil</li><li class=\"d1\">\"on whatever day you eat from it, you shall <b>die by death</b>\" - literal</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew <b>infinitive absolute</b>: \"dying, you will die\"; King James \"surely die\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>tree of life</b> not mentioned here - comes back in ch. 3</li><li class=\"d0\">What he does <b>not</b> say: \"if you disobey, <b>I will kill you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">death as <b>consequence</b>, not penalty - a <b>warning</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"don't put the <b>fork in the light socket</b> - you'll be electrocuted\" - not threatening to electrocute you</li></ul>"},{"t":1198.43,"lv":3,"h":"\"Knowledge of good and evil\" = maturity","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Other uses of the phrase in scripture</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Solomon</b> asks for it as a gift - read as <b>wisdom</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Isaiah</b>, virgin / young woman's child: \"before the child is old enough to <b>choose the good and reject the evil</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Everywhere else = <b>maturity</b>, specifically mental</li><li class=\"d1\">the Western <b>age of accountability</b></li><li class=\"d1\">kids don't understand consequences - fork in the light socket - then at a certain point <b>they come to maturity</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So here: Adam <b>born yesterday</b></li><li class=\"d1\">physically an adult male \"maybe\" - \"not going into whether he had a <b>navel</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't know these things</li><li class=\"d1\">God: don't <b>run ahead and grab that maturity</b> for yourself</li></ul>"},{"t":1334.91,"lv":3,"h":"Knowledge before readiness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Second Temple literature</b> and fathers: tree of knowledge <b>still in paradise</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not evil</b> - nothing God created is evil</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: Adam and his \"soon to exist wife\" take from it <b>before they're ready</b></li><li class=\"d1\">before they can use it <b>responsibly</b></li><li class=\"d0\">When the <b>devil</b> shows up (more next time): he wants to <b>destroy</b> them - knowledge before they understand how to use it</li><li class=\"d0\">Ongoing theme in Genesis: <b>knowledge, technology, innovations of culture</b> not good in and of themselves</li><li class=\"d1\">good when a <b>properly prepared</b> person uses them</li><li class=\"d1\">or <b>incredibly destructive</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>don't hand a five-year-old a firearm</b>\" - not because guns are evil</li><li class=\"d0\">So this is a <b>warning</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Adam portrayed as in one of Christ's genealogies: <b>son of God</b> = <b>child</b></li><li class=\"d1\">being prepared for something</li></ul>"},{"t":1475.86,"lv":2,"h":"2:18-20 - \"Not good to be alone\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.18</b>: \"not good for man to be alone; I will make him a <b>helper comparable to him</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.19-20</b>: God forms <b>animals and birds</b>, brings them to Adam to see what he'd <b>call them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">whatever he called each = its name; but <b>no helper comparable</b> found</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>First time</b> God says something isn't good - everything else \"good\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Modern problem: after ideals of <b>romantic love</b> - \"Adam is <b>lonely</b>, sad\"</li><li class=\"d1\">what's the problem? He's <b>living with God</b> - not alone</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't miss having a woman - \"there aren't any\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Real issue: not good that he's the <b>only human</b></li><li class=\"d1\">need more humans → <b>male and female</b> - \"biological male and biological female\" (\"I'm canceled now\")</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I hope I'm not breaking news\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1639.23,"lv":3,"h":"Naming the animals","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"A lot of <b>Rudyard Kipling</b> read into this\" - through ch. 3 with the snake</li><li class=\"d1\">Adam checking out animals to <b>mate</b> - \"go figure, none of them\" - \"<b>Duh</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">innocent like a child, \"not an idiot\" - and God certainly isn't</li><li class=\"d0\">Out of ch. 1: <b>order</b> and <b>fill with life</b>; humanity continues both tasks</li><li class=\"d1\">he <b>can't</b> fill the world - can't reproduce yet</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Naming</b>: <b>Hittite ruins</b> - reliefs of kings on a <b>throne</b>, holding a <b>rod</b>, <b>naming animals</b> walking past</li><li class=\"d1\">common imagery of <b>authority</b></li><li class=\"d1\">king puts and maintains <b>order</b>; <b>wild animals = symbol of disorder and chaos</b></li><li class=\"d1\">naming, <b>taxonomy</b> = applying order, \"civilizing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Adam can't <b>fill</b> yet - but starts on <b>subduing</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>God is watching</b> - cooperative</li><li class=\"d1\">exercise number one in putting the world in order</li><li class=\"d0\">Then the other problem: <b>making more people</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1808.94,"lv":2,"h":"2:21-22 - The \"rib\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.21</b>: God brought a <b>trance</b> upon Adam, he slept, took one of his <b>ribs</b>, filled in the flesh</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Another episode of <b>Father Stephen Ruins Your Sunday School</b>\": <b>no rib</b> anywhere in the story</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Trance\" - ambiguous whether Adam is alive or dead - more like <b>coma</b>, <b>vegetative state</b></li><li class=\"d1\">clear in Hebrew and Greek; fathers compare <b>Christ in the tomb</b> and Adam, <b>woman and the church</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Word translated rib = <b>side</b></li><li class=\"d1\">other uses: dorsal/ventral sides, <b>halves of a building</b> - symmetrical, opposite halves</li><li class=\"d1\">Adam <b>pulled in two</b>, \"ripped in half\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God fills in the other half</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.22</b>: builds the half he took into a <b>woman</b> - <b>both halves built out</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew <i>ish</i> and <i>isha</i> - woman = \"from man\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Idea: pulled in two, and the two <b>come back together in marriage</b></li><li class=\"d1\">when they do, they <b>produce new life in the world</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1972.84,"lv":2,"h":"2:23-24 - One flesh","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.23</b>: \"<b>bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh</b>\" - \"kind of literal\" given what happened</li><li class=\"d1\">\"she shall be called woman because she was taken out of man\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.24</b>: a man <b>leaves father and mother</b>, joined to his wife, <b>two become one flesh</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>physical reunion</b> - the two pulled apart, now come back together</li></ul>"},{"t":2026.75,"lv":3,"h":"Christ on divorce","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Christ appeals to this: Moses allowed a <b>writ of divorce</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Moses' point: vs <b>abandoning</b> them</li><li class=\"d1\">can't leave her in <b>limbo</b> - still technically married, can't remarry</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>polygamy</b> allowed - man could do whatever; woman stuck</li><li class=\"d1\">writ lets her <b>move on with her life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ: Moses allowed it for <b>hardness of heart</b>; not so <b>from the beginning</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"the two will become one flesh\" - \"<b>what God has joined together, let no man put asunder</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Divorce compared to that connection: two who've become one <b>ripped apart again</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>horrible experience</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Side note: not \"you broke a rule, now you get punished\" - a <b>racking experience of being pulled apart</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God would like to <b>spare us</b> that; there are consequences</li></ul>"},{"t":2139.44,"lv":3,"h":"Penance as healing; widowhood","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox Church: after divorce a period of \"quote unquote <b>penance</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">period of <b>healing</b>, working through damage, bringing people back into the community</li><li class=\"d1\">then they can take the <b>Eucharist</b> again the rest of their life - \"unlike certain other churches I won't name\"</li><li class=\"d1\">question of <b>healing and injury</b>, not enforcing a rule or punishing</li><li class=\"d0\">Same for the <b>widowed</b> - difficult to heal</li><li class=\"d1\">he hasn't had it - \"the way I eat, unlikely I will outlive my wife\"</li><li class=\"d1\">very much like <b>being torn apart</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two becoming one flesh = not just a nice image</li><li class=\"d1\">reflects a <b>reality in how humans are built</b> - described in this imagery of creation</li></ul>"},{"t":2247.09,"lv":2,"h":"2:25 - Naked and not ashamed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.25</b>: both naked, Adam and his wife, <b>not ashamed</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>No concept of shame</b> - image of <b>innocence</b></li><li class=\"d1\">little kids, babies <b>run around naked</b>; a certain age, no longer appropriate - \"thrown out of a Circle K\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not that nudity is gross or reaching an age is evil</li><li class=\"d1\">period of <b>innocence</b>, then you come to the <b>knowledge of good and evil</b> - maturity</li><li class=\"d1\">a thing crossed - not a firm line at a certain age, but a reality \"we all see\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Both at the <b>beginning of a journey</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sets up <b>next chapter</b> - \"most people know\"</li><li class=\"d1\">when we read the actual text it may differ from how it's commonly presented</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not</b> the \"just so story about how the <b>snake lost its legs</b>\"</li></ul>"}],"next":"Next week: <b>Genesis 3</b> - the serpent; text differs from the usual telling, not a \"just so story\""});
